nd.\n\nRob Dennis\n\n@NSMSVikings\n\nFriday, September 6, 2019\n\nDear North Shore Middle School Families,\n\nWe did it! We finished our first week of school. From all accounts, it has been a really exciting and positive beginning of the school year. Even though we have only been here three days, sixth graders are beginning to get more comfortable on their teams and in navigating the hallways, seventh and eighth graders are supporting their younger peers by helping them in the hall, and everyone is holding true to our guiding principle that Everyone Matters, Everyone Cares, Everyone Learns. Take a look at our \"Welcome Back\" video slideshow of the first few days of school. Smiles galore!\n\nSome important information for you as you begin to plan your availability for involvement in our school this month:\n\n  * Club Fair - Thursday, September 12th @ 3pm - Please encourage your child to join a club! It is a great way to make new friends and explore areas of interest.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Socially Intelligent Agents (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227019794_Socially_Intelligent_Agents)\nciteturn3207search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.5 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; These could be developed using a combination of anticipatory learning the-\n\n1 A set of models that anticipate the results of action, including commu-\n\nnicative actions (this roughly corresponds to a model of the world includ-\n\ning other agents). Each model would be composed of several parts: - a\n\ncondition for the action - the nature of the action - the anticipated effect\n\nof the action - (possibly) its past endorsements as to its past reliability\n\n42 Socially Intelligent Agents\n\n2 a set of candidate strategies for obtaining its goals (this roughly corre-\n\nsponding to plans); each strategy would also be composed of several\n\nparts: the goal; the sequence of actions, including branches dependent\n\nupon outcomes, loops etc.; (possibly) its past endorsements as to its past\n\nsuccess.\n\nThese could be developed using a combination of anticipatory learning the-\n\nory [15] as reported in [21] and evolutionary computation techniques. Thus\n\nrather than a process of inferring sub-goals, plans etc. they would be construc-\n\ntively learnt (similar to that in [9] and as suggested by [19]). The language\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSurvey of Large Multimodal Reasoning | PDF | Artificial Intelligence | Intelligence (AI) & Semantics (https://www.scribd.com/document/920972890/Perception-Reason-Think-Plan)\nciteturn3207search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: last year; key components including structured reasoning paradigms, reinforcement learning algorithms (e.g., DPO and ... By addressing these dimensions, future models could achieve human-like contextual adaptability, ... Univg-r1: Reasoning guided universal visual grounding with reinforcement learning, 2025. ... reasoner via data synthesis. arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.19934, 2024a. ... Antgpt: Can large language models help long-term action anticipation from videos?\nsoning chains. Finally, drawing on empirical insights from challenging benchmarks and\nexperimental cases of OpenAI O3 and O4-mini, we discuss the conceptual direction of\nnative large multimodal reasoning models (N-LMRMs), which aim to support scalable,\nagentic, and adaptive reasoning and planning in complex, real-world environments. By\nsynthesizing historical trends and emerging research, this survey aims to clarify the current\nlandscape and inform the design of next-generation multimodal reasoning systems.\n\n## Figure 1: The core path of large multimodal reasoning models\n\n∗\nEqual contribution ‡ Corresponding author, email: hubaotian@[Link]\nCONTENTS\n\nContents\n\n1 Introduction 3\n\n## 2 Evolving Paradigms of Multimodal Reasoning and Discussion 4\n\n## 3 Roadmap of Multimodal R